On 2005-08-12, Effalump wrote:
>
> Everett E. Johnson wrote:
> Incidentally, I was considering what happened and how it might be
> explained in game terms, and it seemed to me that I had essentially
> stumbled upon an amulet of time travel... has anyone considered the
> pros and cons of introducing time travel into the game? I could see it
> working in a variety of ways:
>
Obviously this is near impossible to implement, since it would
practically require a savefile to be created _every_move_. Considering
the time and disk space it takes to save a late game, this would not be
practical. Pity.

> 1. Amulet of time travel. If you die while wearing it, you get returned
> to the point at which you put it on (but the amulet itself would have
> crumbled, perhaps with some message like 'The amulet crumbles - must
> have been a dud'). Anything identified or mapped would of course be
> forgotten on return.
>
I like this. It would make a *great* replacement for the "AoLS. It has
the advantage that it doesn't only protect you from death, but also from
other mishaps like BoH-blowing. And it has the disadvantage that you
wouldn't want to put it on right after finding one, since that would
mean zapping you back in time a *long* way when you actually need it.
Handle with care. Nice balance thingy.

> 2. A time travel trap of some sort (I guess a 'wormhole' would be the
> best description). Would transport you to a moment from the past,
> chosen by the RNG (essentialy, every move the RNG would give a chance
> of, say 1 in 500 that that point would become the new 'past moment' to
> which you would be transported).
>
Nasty. Should be one-way only like polytraps for the player. What if a
monster hits this?

> 3. An artifact or tool. When invoked, you are transported back to the
> past. The precise point in the past could be either:
>      a) When it was last applied
>      OR b) A random time as chosen by the RNG as described above.
>
Hm, seems like with your suggestions it wouldn't even have to be one
savefile per move. Still I think that the effort is too high, but the
idea sounds very nice.

